## 4. Calibrating the 90% number

The existing audit (win_c_emit_compat.oot:0, 8) says: "The C emitter produces code that is mostly portable... 90% portable to mingw without changes; 50% with a thin shim layer; 30% with the full chs_rt_win32_*.c redesign." This is ambiguous on which axis. Calibrated per-axis:

| Axis | Verdict | Why |
|------|---------|-----|
| **c_emit alone** (compiled as a single TU, no chs_rt link) | **100% portable under mingw-w64** | orchestrator verified clean syntax. Deep re-walk finds no further blocker. C99 compound literals, designated initializers, `_Generic`, GCC `({...})`, `__attribute__((used, noinline))`, `__attribute__((constructor))` are all accepted by mingw-w64 gcc. |
| c_emit + chs_rt (40 TUs, no shim) | 30% portable | POSIX calls in chs_rt: fork, setrlimit, prctl, pthread, /proc/self/cmdline, dlfcn (some), socket (some). All linker errors or runtime crashes on mingw without shim. |
| c_emit + chs_rt + chs_rt_win32_*.c shim (per win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot) | 90% portable | 86/95 POSIX sites have direct Windows equivalents. 9 need redesign (Landlock -> Job Object; RLIMIT_NOFILE -> no-op; /dev/null -> NUL; execlp -> CreateProcess; /proc/self/cmdline -> GetCommandLineW; /dev/tty -> CONIN$; clearenv -> no-op; epoll/inotify/prctl residual -> Err). |
| c_emit + chs_rt + shim + 9 Landlock/setrlimit/etc. redesigns | 95-100% portable | The 9 redesigns are the entire remaining gap. None of them touch c_emit. |
| Build script (bash) | 0% portable | Replace with PowerShell or cmd driver. Out of c_emit scope. |
| `nm`-style cap-require check (main.oo:178) | 0% portable | Use dumpbin /SYMBOLS or llvm-readobj. Out of c_emit scope. |

The **"90%" number in the prior audit is correctly calibrated to the third row (c_emit + chs_rt + shim)**. The headline text of the prior audit is consistent with this. No correction needed.

For Phase 1 (cross-compile to Windows), the only axis that matters is the **first row** (c_emit alone compiles), and that is 100%. The chs_rt shim is Phase 2. The 90% number is for Phase 2, not Phase 1.

## 5. process_exec sites (the `sh -c` literal pattern)

| Site | File:line | What | Use of `sh` |
|------|-----------|------|-------------|
| process_exec | c_emit_sys.oo:165-166 | `oo_sys_exec(cap, (int)(sizeof((OoStr[]){oo_str_lit("sh"), oo_str_lit("-c"), a1})/sizeof(OoStr)), (OoStr[]){oo_str_lit("sh"), oo_str_lit("-c"), a1})` | Literal `sh` and `-c` strings emitted as `OoStr[]` arg to `oo_sys_exec`. Compiles clean. Requires `sh` on PATH at runtime. |
| sys_exec | c_emit_lower.oo:166-167 | `oo_sys_exec(cap, (int)(sizeof((OoStr[]){<user argv>})/sizeof(OoStr)), (OoStr[]){<user argv>})` | No `sh` literal. User-supplied argv, e.g. `["ls", "-l"]`. Will try to exec `ls` directly. Compiles clean. |

Only **one** sh-invoking site in the c_emit (c_emit_sys.oo:165, process_exec). The sys_exec lower (c_emit_lower.oo:166) does not hard-code `sh`; the user controls argv. The runtime is what calls `execvp` -- and on Windows that runtime is `chs_rt_sys.c::oo_sys_exec` which is fork+execvp (per the chs_rt audit, win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot:118-120). The shim needs to either detect "argv[0] == sh" and route to `cmd /c argv[2]", or always route through a shell. The shim author picks.

No other "sh" / "bash" / "-c" / "/bin/" literal appears in any c_emit_*.oo file. grep over ooda/oodac/c_emit_*.oo for `"sh"` returns 0 matches outside c_emit_sys.oo:165-166. Same for `"bash"`, `"zsh"`, `"/bin/"`, `"/proc/"`, `"nm "`, `readlink`, `/dev/null`. Confirmed.

## 6. Top 3 risks (new, after deep re-walk)

1. **GCC statement expression `({...})` at c_emit_result.oo:180 (c_try_unwrap).** mingw-w64 gcc supports it; MSVC cl does not. If the roadmap ever needs MSVC (not in scope today, Phase 1 = mingw), this single site must be rewritten as a do/while or static inline. Documented; no code change now.
2. **No c_emit #ifdef gates for `_WIN32` / `__MINGW32__`.** Every c_emit_*.oo emits portable plain C plus two GCC extensions. If a future need forces conditional emit (e.g. a Windows-only intrinsic), there is no scaffolding for it. The c_emit_meta.oo #if defined(__GNUC__) is the only existing platform gate, and it is for the attribute, not for the generated code.
3. **The `oo_sys_exec` argv construction at c_emit_sys.oo:165 (process_exec) and c_emit_lower.oo:166-167 (sys_exec) both use C99 compound literals of `OoStr[]` arrays.** C99 standard, mingw supports. Risk: if Phase 2 picks clang-cl (LLVM-windows), some clang versions have had bugs with sizeof on compound-literal arrays passed to functions. Phase 1 = mingw-gcc, so this is not blocking. Flag for Phase 2 review.

## 7. Top 3 quick wins (new, low-cost shims added now)

These are **process** wins, not code edits. They are checks the compiler/runtime owners can add to the C emit or the chs_rt shim cheaply.

1. **Document the `({...})` site at c_emit_result.oo:180 in a code comment.** Add a one-line comment in c_emit_result.oo near line 180 noting that this is a GCC stmt-expr and depends on gcc/clang. The file already comments many such nuances; this slot is empty. ~3 minutes. Avoids future surprise if the compiler lane ever picks MSVC.
2. **Add the second compound-literal site (c_emit_lower.oo:166-167) to the chs_rt_shim_doxygen.md (when written).** Both sites use the same `(OoStr[]){...}` pattern; both need the same sizeof trick. ~5 minutes.
3. **Add a one-line test to `scripts/win_cross_hello_smoke.oo` (when written) that compiles a fixture exercising `Ok(...)`, `Err(...)`, `process_exec`, and a struct literal.** These cover all the new C99-compound-literal and designated-initializer sites at once. ~10 minutes.

## 8. What was not checked

- I did **not** read the bc_emit, vm_exec, wasm_emit, cli_build, cli_emit, cli_parse, ast_emit_*, check_*, parse_*, lex_*, tc_*, token_*, load_*, diag_* files. Those are out of the C emit lane and belong to other compilers/parsers. The c_emit_*.oo lane is bounded.
- I did **not** read the stragglers `chs_rt_str.c` and `chs_rt_list_set.c` in the runtime. They are not in the umbrella (per win_oodac_feasibility.oot:60-63) and not in the c_emit lane.
- I did **not** re-verify line-by-line that every c_emit_*.oo file compiles clean under mingw-w64. The orchestrator verified the C emit alone syntax-checks under mingw, and my re-walk finds no further blocker in source. The empirical build is for the compiler lane.
- I did **not** re-test `-mwindows` vs `-mconsole`. `-mconsole` is the default for mingw and is correct for a CLI .exe that uses stdout and fgets(stdin). The runtime never opens a window. If a future Windows GUI is desired, that is a separate audit.
- I did **not** enumerate all 40 chs_rt_*.c files in this re-walk; the c_emit lane is the scope. The chs_rt POSIX audit (win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot and its 2nd-opinion win_chs_rt_audit_2nd.oot) own that. Three sites in the runtime matter for the c_emit lane: oo_sys_exec (called by c_emit_sys.oo) and oo_emit_tmp_* (called by c_emit_match_*, c_emit_ident.oo, c_emit_deref.oo, c_emit_arc_2.oo, c_emit_stmt.oo). These are runtime C symbols; their definitions live in chs_rt_emit_tmp.c and chs_rt_sys.c (per win_oodac_feasibility.oot:62, 88-89). The c_emit emits references; the runtime defines them. Both are C99. mingw supports both.
- I did **not** run the byte-deterministic smoke test (Phase 1 acceptance). That is the compiler lane; my lane is read-only research.
- I did **not** re-validate the runtime stragglers `chs_rt_str.c` and `chs_rt_list_set.c`. They are not in the umbrella per the prior feasibility audit; if a Windows port wants them in, they are out-of-band.
